 ental-science\n\nAbstract\n\nFuture society needs a sustainable energy sup
 ply to support economic growth\, and artificial intelligence (AI)-aided\, 
 data-driven approaches are rapidly transforming the energy landscape. Sust
 aining this momentum requires solutions that are both cost effective and e
 nergy efficient. The current approach relies on heuristic methods and inte
 nsive data acquisition\, but resource constraints make rapid deployment di
 fficult. Quantifying returns on investment while accounting for uncertaint
 y and risk adds another major challenge. A trustworthy framework is theref
 ore essential to guide this transformation—but how can such a framework 
 be built?\n\nPhysics-informed framework has been often proposed as a safeg
 uard for the data-driven paradigm. To make this paradigm trustworthy\, how
 ever\, its physics-informed foundation must be reinforced through fundamen
 tal science. This presentation will discuss how empirical reduction and in
 duction can strengthen that foundation through quantification and verifica
 tion. The goal is to ensure that regression-model predictions not only obe
 y fundamental principles but are also quantitatively interpretable. This w
 ould connect controlled laboratory test data with real-world\, live data s
 treams for practical applications. Because limited data transferability ha
 s become a major bottleneck\, a quantitative and verifiable physics-inform
 ed framework could help overcome it and accelerate a sustainable transform
 ation that is both cost-effective and energy-effic
